---
import ContentPage from '../components/ContentPage.astro';
import FaqList from '../components/FaqList.astro';
import RelatedLinks from '../components/RelatedLinks.astro';
import { articleSchemas, evidenceDatasetSchema, type FaqItem } from '../seo';
import { SITE_PAGES } from '../site-content';
import { REPO_URL } from '../consts';

const page = SITE_PAGES.evidence;
const breadcrumbs = [{ name: 'Home', path: '/' }, { name: page.label, path: page.path }];
const faqs: FaqItem[] = [
  {
    question: 'Are these production shopping results?',
    answer: 'No. They are deterministic synthetic probes of the published ranking function. They test ordering behavior and weight drift, not live catalog completeness or current platform access.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Can the audit be reproduced?',
    answer: 'Yes. The generator uses a fixed seed with no clock or unseeded randomness. The repository test regenerates the report and fails when the committed output drifts.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What does a passing audit prove?',
    answer: 'It proves that the tested ranking code is invariant to input order, that sponsored-flag flips cannot improve rank or score, and that measured score deltas match the published weights for the probe set.',
  },
];
const dataset = evidenceDatasetSchema(page);
const schemas = articleSchemas(page, breadcrumbs, faqs, dataset ? [dataset] : []);
---

<ContentPage
  page={page}
  breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs}
  schemas={schemas}
  intro="NorthCinder's committed neutrality audit measures the published ranker with deterministic synthetic probes. Fifty offer sets are each shuffled five times, producing 250 rerankings that must match their canonical order. Ninety-nine sponsored-flag flips must produce no rank improvement and no score change. Eleven single-dimension probes must reproduce the published weight deltas exactly. These tests examine ranking behavior, not live store completeness or merchant truth."
>
  <div class="stat-grid" aria-label="Deterministic audit summary">
    <div class="stat-card"><strong>250</strong><span>input-order rerankings with 0 canonical-order divergences</span></div>
    <div class="stat-card"><strong>99</strong><span>sponsored-flag flips with 0 rank improvements</span></div>
    <div class="stat-card"><strong>11</strong><span>weight probes with 0 measured-delta divergences</span></div>
  </div>

  <h2>Why the audit exists</h2>
  <p>
    A ranking promise is easier to trust when a person can inspect both the rule and a repeatable attempt to
    break it. NorthCinder publishes the scoring function, the sponsorship tier, the reason schema, and a
    generated measurement report. The client uses the same ranking package to verify the order returned by
    the buyer-run aggregation service.
  </p>
  <p>
    The audit is generated with a fixed seed, no wall clock, and no unseeded randomness. Running the generator
    twice produces the same bytes. A repository test regenerates the report and compares it with the committed
    copy, so an unexplained behavior change or stale report becomes a test failure.
  </p>

  <h2>Battery 1: input-order invariance</h2>
  <p>
    Candidate collection can complete in different orders because store requests have different latency.
    Arrival order must not become a hidden preference. The audit creates 50 synthetic offer sets and reranks
    five shuffled copies of each set. All 250 outputs must match the canonical order.
  </p>

  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table class="data-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Battery</th><th>Measurement</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Position shuffle</td><td>50 sets × 5 shuffles</td><td>250 rerankings; 0 divergences</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Sponsored injection</td><td>99 organic-to-sponsored flag flips</td><td>0 improvements; 0 score changes</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Attribute weights</td><td>11 isolated dimensions</td><td>0 expected-versus-measured divergences</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>Battery 2: sponsored-flag injection</h2>
  <p>
    The audit takes organic offers and changes only their sponsored flag. A paying-placement signal must not
    improve score or ranking. Each flipped offer must move into the sponsored tier, remain below all organic
    offers, and carry the required de-prioritization label. Across 99 flips, the committed report records zero
    score changes, zero rank improvements, zero sponsored offers left above an organic offer, and zero missing
    labels.
  </p>

  <h2>Battery 3: published weight deltas</h2>
  <p>
    Eleven probes vary one measured dimension between otherwise identical offers. The observed score change
    must equal the weight published by the ranker. The probes cover relative price, budget compliance,
    required attributes, delivery, availability, merchant trust, stated ethics preferences, and sponsorship.
    The sponsored probe must measure exactly zero because sponsorship changes tier and labeling, not score.
  </p>

  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table class="data-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Dimension</th><th>Expected delta</th><th>Measured delta</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Price, cheapest versus priciest</td><td>+40</td><td>+40</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Over budget versus within budget</td><td>-25</td><td>-25</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Required specification match</td><td>+40</td><td>+40</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Delivery deadline met</td><td>+25</td><td>+25</td></tr>
        <tr><td>In stock versus out of stock</td><td>+25</td><td>+25</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Trusted merchant versus unknown</td><td>+10</td><td>+10</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Flagged merchant versus unknown</td><td>-40</td><td>-40</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Stated ethics preference match</td><td>+8</td><td>+8</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Sponsored flag only</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>What these results do not establish</h2>
  <p>
    Synthetic probes do not show that a live store returned every relevant product, that a seller supplied a
    correct sponsorship label, that credentials remain approved, or that a real purchase completed. They do
    not measure user satisfaction, market adoption, merchant safety, or the quality of every possible brief.
    NorthCinder therefore reports provenance, trust evidence, and store coverage alongside the score.
  </p>
  <p>
    The audit also does not make a universal claim that the weights are morally or personally correct. They
    are public policy choices that a buyer can inspect, test, and disagree with. The measurable claim is that
    the current implementation applies those choices deterministically to the disclosed inputs.
  </p>

  {REPO_URL && <p>
    Reproduce the results from the <a href={`${REPO_URL}/blob/main/docs/NEUTRALITY-AUDIT.md`}>generated audit</a>,
    inspect the <a href={`${REPO_URL}/blob/main/packages/protocol/src/ranking/neutrality-audit.ts`}>audit generator</a>,
    and compare the <a href={`${REPO_URL}/blob/main/packages/protocol/src/ranking/rank.ts`}>ranking source</a>.
  </p>}

  <FaqList items={faqs} />
  <RelatedLinks links={[
    { href: '/ranking/', label: 'Read the ranking contract', detail: 'Connect the measured probes to the published ordering rules.' },
    { href: '/checkout-safety/', label: 'Inspect the authority boundary', detail: 'See how recommendation and purchase remain separate.' },
    { href: '/store-coverage/', label: 'Understand evidence limits', detail: 'Separate adapter behavior from current platform access.' },
  ]} />
</ContentPage>
